### 1. Add the {{ .Name }} extension image to your Cluster

Follow the [quick start](../../README.md#quick-start) to set up extension-update email alerts
first, then use the CronJob to install and refresh the catalog.

Define the `{{ .Name }}` extension under the `postgresql.extensions` section of
your `Cluster` resource. For example:

<!--
If preloads or PostgreSQL parameters are required, add a short, separate
paragraph immediately before this YAML and show the same settings in it.
Distinguish optional settings from requirements. Keep required settings here,
not under caveats or verification; refer to upstream documentation for tuning.
Omit this paragraph when no settings are needed; do not state that preloading
is unnecessary.
-->

```yaml
apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1
kind: Cluster
metadata:
  name: cluster-{{ .Name }}
spec:
  imageCatalogRef: ...

  postgresql:
    extensions:
    - name: {{ .Name }}
```

### 2. Enable the extension in a database

<!--
If this extension has prerequisite extensions, name them here. When installation
order must be controlled, replace the two installation options below with a SQL
example that installs the prerequisites first, then this extension, in the same
database. Use IF NOT EXISTS for prerequisites that may already be installed.
CNPG does not automatically resolve extension dependencies. Keep dependency
installation order here; section 1 should include any required extension images
in the Cluster YAML. Prerequisites supplied by the base image need no extra image.
-->

Once the extension's files are available in the cluster, you can install it in a
database in either of two ways:

- **Declaratively**, using a [CloudNativePG `Database` resource](https://cloudnative-pg.io/docs/1.30/declarative_database_management/#managing-extensions-in-a-database).
- **Through SQL**, by running `CREATE EXTENSION {{ replaceAll .Name "-" "_" }};` in the target database.

For extensions that normally require superuser privileges, platform teams can use
[pgextwlist](../../pgextwlist/README.md) to allow non-superusers to install an
administrator-approved selection of extensions.

### 3. Verify installation

